# Games Plan Documents — Sync Rule

Two planning projects exist as documentation only. When implementation begins, or when
any new information changes an assumption, **the documents must be updated in the same
piece of work as the code.**

| Folder | Covers |
|---|---|
| `New games plan/` | Stage 0 defect fixes, then the in-house game-module architecture |
| `External game plans/` | Integrating games from a third-party skill-game provider |

## THE SCENARIO IS DECIDED - EXTERNAL-ONLY (owner, 2 September 2026)

**Provider games are the only new games. No in-house game is built.** The active
programme is `External game plans/` and its **X0-X12** phases. Read
`External game plans/PROGRESS.md` first.

| Scenario | Meaning | Chapters | Programme | Status |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **External-only** | Provider games are the **only** new games. No in-house game | `01`-`09` **and** `10`-`20` | X0-X12, **23-30 weeks** | **CHOSEN** |
| **Add-on** | The `New games plan` is delivered too. External games are an addition | `01`-`09` | E1-E9, 7.5-8 weeks on top of the foundation | **Not pursued.** Kept only so E-phase references resolve |

Chapters `10`-`20` exist because `01`-`09` delegated the foundation and every
platform-wide change (admin, navigation, wording, flags, catalogue, risks, rollout) to the
`New games plan`. That plan is not being delivered, so the dependency did not disappear -
it moved into `External game plans/`, and `10`-`20` own it.

**What the decision does to the `New games plan` folder**, because "superseded" is too
blunt: **Stage 0 / `00a` is fully active and unchanged** - it is X0, a prerequisite in
every scenario, and the work currently awaiting owner test. **P1 survives as X1**, same
seams, delivered from `External game plans/11`. **P2, the in-house Trivia game, is not
being built.** **P3-P7 are delivered as X6-X11.** The chapters remain valuable as analysis
of the live codebase, and `15-platform-transformation-and-gaps.md` is still the record of
the games-first direction.

**The finding to keep repeating, because the intuition is wrong:** external-only is **not
cheaper**, it is broader. Dropping an in-house game saves ~3.5 weeks; a correct provider
integration costs ~7; all the platform work is identical. The prize is a catalogue and no
content burden, not a saving. Do not let a summary anywhere imply otherwise.

**The consequence of the decision that must stay visible:** there is now **no in-house
game to fall back on**. If the provider search or the pricing fails, the platform will
have funded the whole foundation, admin and player programme and still have one game. That
is risk **X8**, and it is why open question 10 - a small in-house hedge game - moved
*forward* to before X4 rather than away.

### Two owner instructions that came with the decision

Both change the order rather than the contents, and both are load-bearing.

- **Admin first.** An operator must be able to register a provider, sync a catalogue and
  create a non-trading contest before any player screen has something real to render. The
  admin app is also a separate process with no player traffic, so it is the safe place to
  be wrong. **It does not mean X6 moves in front of X1-X5** - a contest must exist before
  it can be administered. In practice it means X6 and X6.5 complete *before* X7 begins.
- **One step at a time, without breaking the running app.** Every phase additive and
  flag-gated, trading pinned by the regression test in `11`, and the rule that **no phase
  may require a simultaneous change to trading in order to be correct.** If one appears
  to, the seam in `11` is wrong and that is the thing to fix.

### What the owner's 2 September brief added, and what only looked new

Most of the brief was already designed. Four things were not, and the distinction is the
useful part - a plan that claims to cover everything is the one that surprises you.

| Genuinely new | Already designed - cross-reference, do not rewrite |
|---|---|
| **Smart onboarding and interest matchmaking** - nothing at all in `01`-`19`. New chapter `20`, phase **X11.5**, 2-3 weeks | Admin nav grouping with a TRADING group (`12` s1), provider registration and credentials (`04` s3.1, `12` s4), catalogue as editable content (`16` s1), cross-game scoring in three layers (`05`), financials by game and provider (`12` s5), game pages and filtered browsing (`16`, `13` s4), terminology token layer (`14`) |
| **"Challenge any user"** - `03` designed the challenge *mechanics* then said the opponent was "invited or matched" and stopped. X10 grew 0.5-1 week to 1-1.5 | Picking the *game* for a challenge was fully designed |
| **A player profile specification** - it existed only as "terminology pass 6, profile tabs", treating a structural change as a labelling one. `13` s7.2 | Dashboard, leaderboard and `UserGameStats` |
| **The admin wording pass was in the wrong phase** - it sat in X8, *after* the player UI, which would have had operators running a multi-game platform through screens labelled "trading" for two phases. Split into **X6.5** (admin) and X8 (player); `14` total unchanged at ~10 days | The token dictionary, the passes themselves, the never-rename list |

**Net: 23-30 weeks, up from 20-26.** ~3 weeks of real scope, not a re-estimate. Do not let
a summary present it as the same programme.

### Five findings from that pass that generalise

- **A trading-shaped service does not fail when a second game arrives - it keeps working
  and keeps being wrong.** `lib/services/matchmaking.service.ts` and
  `GET /api/matchmaking` already exist and rank opponents by *trading* skill. Add a second
  game and it returns matches happily, all of them trading matches, with no error, no
  empty state and no log line. Same shape as the mirror-drift and `canEnterChallenges`
  defects: **the system reports success while doing the wrong thing.** The rule:
  **change the service, do not call it from a new place**, and prove it with a test that
  fails first. Risk **X13**. The same trap sits in
  `components/dashboard/GettingStartedCard.tsx`, whose steps tell every new player to
  "place your first trade".
- **"Like payment providers" was right about the UX and wrong about the storage.** The
  owner asked for games to be added the way payment providers are, and
  `PaymentProvidersSection.tsx` is indeed the right interaction model. But
  `payment-provider.model.ts` embeds `credentials[]` in the document every screen reads and
  carries a `saveToEnv` flag with a `regenerate-env` route that writes secrets to `.env`.
  `04` s3.1 deliberately keeps game credentials **out** of `game_provider`. Copying the
  pattern wholesale would have undone that on consistency grounds. **Copy the UX, not the
  persistence** - `12` s4.1.
- **A menu group is not a tab.** `12` s1 already grouped the trading sections, but the
  owner asked for one destination with tabs inside. Two things must survive the collapse:
  **deep links** (tab state must be addressable, not `useState`, or every bookmark and
  wiki screenshot breaks), and **RBAC** (`ADMIN_SECTIONS` grants per *section*, so merging
  six into one is a silent privilege widening that looks completely correct on review).
  **Keep the six IDs and gate the tabs.** `12` s1.1.
- **No aggregate may silently mean "trading only".** Now a binding rule in `05` s10: every
  performance or money figure is either generalised across games, explicitly scoped and
  labelled to one game, or removed from the platform-wide view. There is no third option.
  **"Total PnL" is the clearest casualty** - a chess puzzle has no profit and loss, so it
  moves inside the trading card or becomes something genuinely cross-game. The failure mode
  is not a crash: a trading-only total keeps computing and keeps rendering.
- **Inference is not consent.** Paying to enter a competition says a player will *play* a
  game; it does not say they want 1v1 invitations from strangers. `20` s3.2 keeps them
  separate - inferred interest drives **suggestions**, an explicit opt-in drives
  **invitations**. Conflating them turns matchmaking into spam. Risk **X14**. The same
  distinction made `canEnterChallenges` a live defect: **a stored value and an absent one
  are different facts.**

**Stage 0 is a prerequisite in both scenarios.** It fixes live defects under the money
layer, and nothing changes that.

**Stage 0 started on 1 September 2026** and is tracked in `New games plan/PROGRESS.md`,
which is the file to read before touching any of this. Three things from it are easy to
get wrong and are worth stating here:

- **Prerequisite A already shipped** (commit `d5d3a328`): the `/api/simulator/*` routes
  accepted a plain `X-Simulator-Mode: true` header as authentication in production, so an
  unauthenticated caller could credit any wallet. Recorded as **R27** in
  `External game plans/17-risk-register.md` because the provider callback specified in
  `01`/`06` can repeat the same mistake. Two rules follow: **an authentication helper must
  never accept a request because configuration is missing**, and **test the route, not just
  the helper.**
- **The finalize-time prize-pool safeguard does not mask the join-path defect.** It only
 fires when the pool is too *high*. An under-counted pool is under-distributed silently.
 Several documents used to claim the opposite; if you find that claim, it is stale.
- **Model mirror drift is a WRITE-side defect, with one read-side trap.** Measured against
 a real MongoDB on 1 Sep 2026 (`__tests__/helpers/mirror-drift-behaviour.test.ts`, 8
 tests). An ordinary `save()` does **not** strip fields the schema lacks, and `.lean()` /
 `toObject()` do **not** hide them — several documents used to claim both, and both are
 stale. What actually happens: a **missing enum value rejects the whole write**, and the
 app with the narrower copy **cannot write the field at all**, silently, while reporting
 success. **The exception, and the subtlest part: ordinary `doc.field` access returns
 `undefined`**, because Mongoose defines getters only for declared paths — so the field
 survives a debug dump while the code beside it reads nothing and takes the wrong branch.
 Two rules follow: **compare enum values, not just field names**, and **never cite a read
 as evidence that two copies agree.**
- **Defect 2 is built** (1 Sep 2026, awaiting owner test): the guard in
 `tools/model-mirror/`, all 11 drifted pairs synced, 20 tests, and the
 `mongodb-memory-server` replica-set harness the money tests need. It found **three** live
 production bugs on the way — failed withdrawals recording no time or reason, six
 landing-page sections that could not be edited at all, and hero/branding images that
 could never be restored after a redeploy. The third was found by **diffing the typecheck
 against a pre-sync baseline** (admin app 229 → 225 errors); errors that *disappear* after
 a sync mark code that was already reaching for a field its schema denied it. Worth
 repeating whenever models are synced.
- **The order is Defect 2 before Defect 1**, so the mirror guard exists before Defect 1
 edits the admin copy of the entry path.
- **Three owner decisions were taken on 1 Sep 2026** and must not be quietly reversed:
 the test database is **`mongodb-memory-server` as a single-node replica set** (built and
 proven with a real transaction); the orphaned declaration files are **deleted**; and
 **joining a competition is allowed outside market hours — only trading itself is gated.**
 That last one changes the Defect 1 design: the unified `contest-entry.service.ts` takes
 the union of the *security* checks from both gates and deliberately does **not** carry
 Gate B's market-hours check, because doing so would block weekend sign-ups for Monday
 contests. Locked in by Defect 1 test 12, which fails both a fix that blocks the join and
 one that lets the order through.
- **`apps/admin` has its own `node_modules/mongoose`.** Admin models therefore run on a
 **separate Mongoose instance** which a test's `mongoose.connect` does not touch. The
 symptom is `Operation "x.insertOne()" buffering timed out after 10000ms`, which reads like
 a slow database rather than an unconnected one. Connect it via **`Model.base`**, not a
 guessed path — that is the instance the model registered on, and it survives npm hoisting.
 Second rule from the same area: **never import both copies of one model into a single
 test.** Both register under the same name via `models.X || model(...)`, so the second
 import silently returns the first and the test examines the wrong schema while looking
 correct.
- **A synced schema is not a working feature — the `brandingFiles` bug proved it twice.**
 Found and **fixed** 1 Sep 2026. The upload routes key the map by filename and **Mongoose
 refuses map keys containing a dot**. The sync changed the failure mode without removing it:
 before, the undeclared field meant a plain JavaScript `Map` accepted the key and the write
 was silently discarded; after, `default: new Map()` hands the route a `MongooseMap` that
 validates and throws, and the route catches it and warns while still reporting success.
 **No entry had ever been stored, so there was nothing to migrate.** Fixed by
 `lib/utils/branding-file-key.ts` (mirrored into `apps/admin`), encoding `.` as `__DOT__`,
 wired through **two writers, four readers and one delete** across both apps. Two rules
 follow: **the mirror guard proves two copies agree, not that either one works** — follow a
 drift fix with a test that round-trips real data; and **assert the round trip through the
 real helper, never a hard-coded encoded string**, or a reader that forgot to encode still
 passes.
- **Every test that recorded a defect was flipped to prove its fix, not deleted** (1 Sep
 2026, three bugs). Keep doing this. The comment that explained why the defect was reachable
 is the most valuable part of the test, and deleting it loses the reason the guard exists.
 Rename the test, invert the assertion, keep the history in the `// Reason:` block.
- **Two fixes deliberately stopped short, and both are recorded rather than silently
 scoped.** `cancelCompetitionAndRefund`'s lock is `status !== "cancelled"`, so a **completed**
 competition is still refundable — entry fees returned on top of prizes already paid. And
 `error instanceof Error ? error.message` in a route response appears **166 times across 119
 API route files**; only the proven one was changed. In both cases the wider change is a
 policy decision or an unreviewable diff, so it belongs with the unified service. **Do not
 let a summary imply either is closed.**
- **Setting the final status up front IS the lock.** The double-refund fix needed no new enum
 value: `findOneAndUpdate({ _id, status: { $ne: "cancelled" } }, { $set: { status:
 "cancelled", ... } })` claims and completes in one instruction, and a transaction abort rolls
 it back for a retry. An intermediate `cancelling` state would have meant a schema change in
 both apps for no gain. Also: **a duplicate money request should return success with a zero
 count**, not an error — a retried cron delivery has done nothing wrong, and a failure invites
 a third attempt.
- **Finalization recomputes participant stats from trades before ranking.** Seeding `pnl` on a
 `CompetitionParticipant` does nothing; it is overwritten with zero, every player ties at rank
 1, and the resulting equal payout **reads as a prize-distribution bug in production**. To
 rank participants, seed a **closed `TradingPosition` and a matching `TradeHistory` row** —
 the realized amount is read from the history by `positionId`, not derived from the position.
 Use **USD-quoted symbols** or finalization fetches conversion rates over the network, and
 satisfy the **whole** competition schema (`createdBy`, `registrationDeadline`, `description`),
 because finalization saves the document even though those fields play no part in a payout.
- **The challenge accept route was missing the restriction and fraud guards** (proven and
 then **fixed** 1 Sep 2026, `__tests__/services/challenge-accept-guards.test.ts`,
 sub-defect 1b). It checked seven other guards. The challenge **create** route checks both,
 so only accept was affected — which is why reading one route would never have found it. A
 challenge is the easiest shape for coordinated entry, being exactly two players with the
 pot returning to the pair minus the platform fee, so the fraud gap mattered here at least
 as much as on a competition. See the fix note below.
- **Classify concurrency failures, never merely count them.** Asserting that the artifact
 and unknown buckets are *empty* is what stops test-server noise inflating a finding. And
 note the trap in the other direction: a genuine write conflict reads `Write conflict during
 plan execution **and yielding is disabled**`, so matching "yielding is disabled" as an
 infrastructure marker files every real failure as noise and makes the defect vanish.
- **`ensureCollections()` alone is not enough — indexes are a catalog change too.**
 Mongoose builds a model's indexes on first use, which produces the same `due to catalog
 changes` failure inside a transaction. That residue made the sequential control test pass
 alone and fail about **one full-suite run in three**. `settleIndexes()` awaits
 `Model.init()` for every registered model and `ensureCollections()` calls it. A test that
 fails only when the suite is busy teaches the team to rerun rather than to read.
- **Before writing any concurrency test, call `ensureCollections()`** from
 `__tests__/helpers/mongo-test-server.ts` with every collection the code under test writes.
 MongoDB **cannot create a collection inside a transaction**, so the first test to touch a
 new collection fails with `due to catalog changes` — which is indistinguishable at a glance
 from a contention failure. This nearly put a false finding on record: a 20-way join test
 reported 1 success in 20, and most of the failures were this artifact. Measured after that
 artifact was removed, Gate A admitted **1 concurrent join in 20** because it had no retry
 while Gate B retried five times; **since the unified service gave both gates the retry, the
 same test measures 20 in 20.** The ratio is not a production prediction either way, because
 a single-node in-memory replica set has tighter lock timeouts. Assert against the *observed*
 success count, never against the attempt count, so the test survives the fix.
- **Test fixtures must use ObjectId-shaped user ids** (24 hex chars), not readable labels.
 Better Auth uses the MongoDB adapter so real ids are ObjectId strings; most models type
 `userId` as `String` and would accept anything, but the fraud models declare
 `Schema.Types.ObjectId` and throw a `CastError` that the entry path catches and logs. The
 test then passes while a whole branch of the code under test never runs — coverage that
 only looks like coverage.
- **Testing a server action needs `__tests__/helpers/server-action-context.ts`**, which
 supplies the session, headers, restriction and fraud outcomes that ambient request state
 would normally provide. Two things will waste your time otherwise. Server actions
 **return** `{ success: false, error }` rather than throwing, so assert on the result, not
 with `rejects.toThrow`. And they re-throw only errors whose **`digest` starts with
 `NEXT_`** — a `redirect()` mock without a digest is swallowed into a result object, the
 redirect appears never to fire, and **the production code looks broken when it is
 correct.** Every guard test must assert the refusal left **no participant, no debit and no
 prize-pool change**, not merely the right message.
- **Defect 1's entry-fee ledger defect was proven, not inferred, and is now fixed** (1 Sep
 2026, `__tests__/services/entry-fee-ledger.test.ts`). Gate A wrote `referenceId`, which the
 `WalletTransaction` schema does not declare, and never set `competitionId`, which it
 does — so **every competition entry fee was unattributable to its competition.** Two
 things to state accurately: the harm was a **broken audit trail, not a wrong balance**
 (nothing computes money from that field — checked), and because *both* copies wrote
 `referenceId` identically this was **one bug duplicated, not mirror drift**, so the guard
 does not catch it and cannot. The unified service writes `competitionId`; historical rows
 stay unattributable, so **do not describe the audit trail as repaired retroactively.**
- **The same defect existed on the challenge side, wider, and was fixed 1 Sep 2026.**
 `challengeId` was declared on **neither** `WalletTransaction` copy while **nine** writers
 passed it — challenge entry (×2), the decline refund, and six finalization payouts across
 both apps — so **the entire challenge money trail was unattributable**, not just entry.
 Same harm statement as its sibling: audit trail, not balance; nothing queries
 `wallettransactions` by it; nothing to backfill, because the value never reached the
 database. Declared add-only in both copies with a matching index, proven by
 `__tests__/services/challenge-accept-guards.test.ts` and probed by un-declaring it again.
 **This is the third "one bug duplicated, not drift" case**, after `referenceId` and
 `failedReason`, so carry the general rule rather than the three instances: **the mirror
 guard proves the two apps agree with each other, never that either agrees with the code
 writing to them.** A green `check:mirrors` is not evidence that a field is stored.
- **Verify the throwaway sentence, not just the claim you set out to test.** That bug was
 found only because a passing remark inside a paragraph about something else — asserting
 that `challengeId` *was* declared, and that `competitionId` was an ObjectId, both false —
 was checked before being left on record. Documentation written during implementation is a
 defect-finding instrument; an unverified aside in it deserves the same suspicion as an
 unverified claim in code.
- **Defect 1 is built** (1 Sep 2026, awaiting owner test): `lib/services/contest-entry.service.ts`
 holds the transaction, with the result shapes, the pre-transaction guards and the
 post-commit work in `lib/services/contest-entry/` (`types.ts`, `guards.ts`,
 `side-effects.ts`) to keep every file under the 500-line limit. `enterCompetition` and
 `POST /api/competitions/[id]/join` are now thin wrappers over it, and the service re-exports
 the types so importers only ever need the one path.
 **The first thing to get right is that there were four
 writers, not the two the plan described** — the two known gates, a simulator batch route,
 and a dead admin copy. Each was resolved differently and the reasoning matters: the
 **dead admin copy was deleted** (nothing imported it, and it carried weaker guards that a
 future feature would have inherited); the **batch route was fixed in place** rather than
 routed through the service, because its bulk insert is the point of it. **Count the writers
 before unifying anything** — a plan that says "two paths" is an assumption until grep says so.
- **The batch route silently dropped six participant fields**, because it built seats with
 names the `CompetitionParticipant` schema does not declare (`pnlPercent` for
 `pnlPercentage`, `joinedAt` for `enteredAt`, and four more). Two rules: **extract an
 inline object builder into a named function before testing it** — the seat shape is now
 `lib/services/simulator/simulator-participant.ts` and a test compares its keys against
 `Model.schema.paths`, which no assertion on the saved document could do, since strict mode
 has already discarded the evidence by then. And **prove a field-name test fails** by
 injecting a bad name and watching it go red; a test that only ever passes proves nothing.
- **A duplicate entry is idempotent success, not an error** (owner decision, 1 Sep 2026):
 return the existing seat and take no second fee. That covers a double-click and a retried
 request. It needs handling in **two** places, and missing either leaves the hole open — the
 pre-flight participant check, *and* the **E11000 duplicate-key error** from the unique index
 when two requests pass the check in the same instant. Treat E11000 on that index as success.
- **`SuspicionScore` had two independent races, both now fixed** (1 Sep 2026,
 `__tests__/services/suspicion-score-race.test.ts`). It surfaced only once the entry path
 stopped serialising itself, which is the general lesson: **removing a bottleneck exposes
 every race the bottleneck was hiding** — the old path admitted 1 concurrent join in 20, so
 the losers never reached the fraud services at all. Five call sites, not the three first
 counted, each read-then-created its own document against a unique index; **17 of 20**
 concurrent detectors threw `E11000` and their contributions were discarded silently,
 because every caller is fire-and-forget. Fixed by making `getOrCreateScore` an atomic
 upsert and routing all five through it.
 **The second race is the more instructive one, and the upsert did not fix it.** `save()`
 writes only the paths one caller modified, so two detectors using *different* methods each
 persisted their own breakdown entry while `totalScore` — a whole-document field both
 computed from a stale copy — was last-write-wins. The document then **contradicted its own
 breakdown**: 40 and 25 stored correctly, total 40, so `riskLevel` sat at medium when 65
 crosses "high" and the account was never auto-restricted. Two rules follow: **a derived
 total is not safe just because its inputs are on separate paths**, and the fix is to
 recompute it **server-side from the persisted document** with a `$set` aggregation-pipeline
 update, which is order-independent and therefore converges no matter how the callers
 interleave. Two remaining read-then-create sites in `scripts/` are deliberately left —
 they are hand-run and single-threaded.
- **Sub-defect 1b is fixed** (1 Sep 2026): challenge accept now calls
 `checkAccountStanding` from `lib/services/contest-entry/guards.ts`, shared with the
 unified entry service so the two paid entry paths cannot drift apart again. Two things to
 keep right. It must pass **`enterChallenge`, never `enterCompetition`** — the flags differ
 on purpose, since `canEnterChallenges` blocks only on an explicit `false` so that
 restrictions predating the field stay allowed, and swapping them would quietly refuse every
 legacy-restricted account. And the guard runs **before any wallet read**, so a refusal
 cannot leave one of the two debits applied. Pinned by a test that asserts *which* action
 the route asks about, not merely that it refused — the refusal alone passes either way.
 Swept the sibling routes: challenge **create** already had both, **decline** and the admin
 challenge route only ever credit, so there is nothing else to fix.
- **Prerequisite B is fixed** (2 Sep 2026, awaiting owner test), and it exists because the
 owner reported a live incident rather than because anything in the plan predicted it: a
 player was locked out of paid entry by their **suspicion score alone**. The rule that comes
 out of it is short and load-bearing — **scores raise alerts, restrictions block.** The entry
 gate used to return `RISK_SCORE_BLOCKED` above `entryBlockThreshold`, which created no
 `UserRestriction`, so it appeared on no admin screen, notified nobody, could not be lifted,
 and **read `entryBlockThreshold` while never looking at `autoSuspendEnabled`** — automatic
 lockouts while the admin had automatic suspension switched off. The subtlest part is why it
 was unreportable: the dashboard's account-status card renders only while an alert is
 *pending or investigating*, so **dismissing the alert deleted the last explanation while
 the block stayed.** `entryBlockThreshold` is now a **Review** threshold (name kept in the
 schema, documented as historical — renaming it is a mirrored migration for a cosmetic gain),
 the dead `shouldBlockEntry` helper is deleted in both apps, and elevate-to-investigation now
 offers the full suspension options with the player notified either way. **Do not reintroduce
 a refusal inside the gate.** If automatic action is wanted, raise a restriction, so it stays
 reversible.
- **Five more live defects were found under Prerequisite B, and two generalise.** The
 first: `app/api/fraud/suspicion-score/route.ts` in the **player** app let any signed-in user
 read the high-risk list, **raise a rival's score to lock them out of a paid competition**,
 or clear their own — all three handlers commented "admin only", none checking anything but
 that a session existed. Deleted, since nothing called it and the admin app's copy is
 guarded. **That is the third time a comment has claimed an authorization check the code
 never performed**, after Prerequisite A and the internal-secret fallbacks, so carry it as a
 class: **a comment asserting authorization is not evidence one runs, and a route nothing
 calls is still reachable over HTTP.** The second generalisable one: auto-suspension set
 `suspensionEndsAt`, which `UserRestriction` does not declare, so strict mode discarded it
 and an unset `expiresAt` means **permanent** on this model — every auto-suspension was
 permanent while its reason text and history entry both promised seven days. **Same failure
 mode as mirror drift, from a different direction — one writer disagreeing with the schema
 rather than two copies disagreeing with each other — and `check:mirrors` cannot catch it.**
 The other three: **Auto-Suspend was near-unreachable at its default**, because it ran only
 on a risk-*band* change (bands 30/50/70) while `autoSuspendThreshold` defaults to 90, so a
 score entering critical at 72 was correctly refused for being under 90 and never examined
 again however high it climbed; **suspended and banned accounts could still accept paid 1v1
 challenges**, since `canEnterChallenges` was the only permission flag defaulting to
 *allowed* and 10 of 11 restriction writers never set it; and **`resetScore()` recorded every
 delta as `-0`**, computing `-this.totalScore` after zeroing the total.
- **Fix a wrong default at the schema, not across the call sites — but check for the one
 caller that meant it.** `canEnterChallenges` was wrong in 10 of 11 writers, so flipping the
 default fixed ten places with one line. The eleventh, the duplicate-KYC path driven by
 `duplicateKYCBlockChallenges`, sets the flag deliberately, and it is the reason the
 migration in `tools/fraud/fix-entry-blocked-users.ts --close-challenge-hole` filters on
 `reason: { $ne: "kyc_fraud" }`. Two rules follow. **A schema default fixes future rows
 only** — Mongoose already persisted the old value, so existing documents hold a real stored
 `true` indistinguishable from intent; the migration is not optional. And **a test that pins
 the new default must be accompanied by one pinning the explicit override**, or the two
 behaviours collapse into one assertion: probing this turned exactly 1 test red while the
 explicit-intent test stayed green, which is what proved they were independent.
- **A dead helper is an invitation, so delete it with the defect.** `shouldBlockEntry()`
 existed in both `fraud-settings.service.ts` copies, was called by nothing after the gate fix,
 and did exactly the thing that had just been removed. Leaving it would have made
 reintroducing the defect a one-line change that reads like using an existing API. Same
 reasoning as deleting the unprotected route rather than guarding it.

### The first X-phase code shipped 2 September 2026, and four things from it generalise

The admin navigation restructure (`12` s1/s1.1, built and recorded in **s1.1a**) separated
contest administration from trading and collapsed the six trading screens into one
collapsible **Trading** destination inside a **GAMES** group. It is a slice of **X6 taken
deliberately before X1 and before Stage 0 sign-off**, on the grounds that it is the only
part of X6 with no dependency on the foundation — it reorganises existing screens and adds
no field, model, service, route or server action — and that the admin app carries no player
traffic. **That reasoning does not extend to anything which writes data.**

- **A restatement can be wrong while its source is right, and that is the direction nobody
 checks.** `12` restated `New games plan/07`'s admin grouping and introduced two errors:
 an **Arsenal** tab that is not an admin section at all (`TradingArsenalContext.tsx` is
 chart tooling with no `menuGroups` entry and no `ADMIN_SECTIONS` id), and a **missing
 `market-data`**, which is real. `07` had both right. The paired-document table is written
 as though drift flows source → restatement; it flows both ways, so **verifying the source
 is not verifying the chapter you are building from.** Concretely: **a plan's list of
 screens is a hypothesis until checked against `menuGroups` and `ADMIN_SECTIONS`
 together** — presence in one but not the other is the interesting case, because the
 section can then either not be granted or not be reached.
- **Before building a mechanism a plan demands, check whether the codebase already has
 it.** Both of `12`'s hard cautions — tab state must be addressable, RBAC must stay
 per-section — were **already satisfied**. Sections are driven by `?activeTab=<sectionId>`,
 so reusing section ids as tab ids preserved every deep link with no new code; and
 `filteredMenuGroups` already reveals a parent only when `children.some(hasAccess)` and
 filters the children individually, which *is* per-tab gating, already proven by `settings`
 and `dev-zone-menu`. The `useState` trap the plan warned about is only reachable by adding
 a **second** state beside the one that exists. **The caution was right and the remedy was
 to reuse, not to build.**
- **A menu parent that renders nothing must not be a permission.** `trading-menu` is
 deliberately absent from `ADMIN_SECTIONS`. A grant that maps to no screen is exactly where
 privilege widening starts, and it reviews as harmless. Related and load-bearing:
 `ADMIN_SECTIONS` is a **Mongoose enum** on `allowedSections` and `customPermissions`, so
 it is **add-only** — removing a value orphans every employee document storing it. Note
 `admin-employee.model.ts` lives **only** in `apps/admin` and is *not* mirrored, so
 `check:mirrors` says nothing about it.
- **Classify a screen by the component it renders, never by the group it is filed under.**
 `analytics` sat in the "Trading" group and renders `CompetitionAnalytics` — contest
 analytics, which belongs to every game. When the group's name is the thing being
 corrected, that name is the least reliable evidence available. Same trap as the
 trading-shaped services in `matchmaking.service.ts`: **the label agrees with the old world
 and keeps agreeing after it ends.**

Pinned by `__tests__/admin/trading-section-nav.test.ts` (9 tests), **both halves probed by
reintroducing the defect** — restoring `trading-risk` under Settings turned exactly 1 red,
dropping a tab from the shared list turned 2 red. Verified by a full admin `next build` and
a `tsc --noEmit` matching the **225-error baseline exactly**, with none in the changed files
and, equally important, **none disappearing**. Still deferred: sidebar clicks do not write
the URL (pre-existing, affects all ~60 sections, belongs with X6.5), and the
`tradingEnabled` conditional that hides the destination waits on X1 — **"built" does not
include it.**

## The rule

**Documentation is part of the deliverable, not a follow-up task.** A phase is not
finished until the documents match what was actually built.

On every change during implementation:

1. **Update `PROGRESS.md`** in the relevant folder — status table, work log entry,
   decisions, open questions. This is the file a new chat reads first.
2. **Update the affected chapter(s).** If the data model changed, `04-data-model.md`
   changes. If a flow changed, the flow document changes.
3. **Update the HTML versions**, which are generated from the same content and drift
   silently otherwise:
   - `New games plan/ChartVolt-New-Games-Plan.html` — internal
   - `New games plan/ChartVolt-New-Games-Plan-Partners.html` — partner-facing
   - `External game plans/ChartVolt-External-Games-Plan.html` — internal
   - `External game plans/ChartVolt-Game-API-Requirements.html` — sent to providers
4. **Record deviations explicitly.** If the build differed from the plan, say so in the
   work log with the reason. Never silently rewrite the plan to match the code.
5. **Check whether docs outside these folders are affected** — `README.md`,
   `.cursor/rules/*.mdc`, `deploy/README.md`, the admin wiki
   (`apps/admin/components/admin/AdminWikiSection.tsx`), and
   `legal/ChartVolt-Regulatory-Defence-Pack.html` if anything touches the
   skill-versus-chance argument or the money flow.

